{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792417524,"nonce":"GpLp0y/uzKmDUvvfMeN7xyUl7is=","title":"Redraw only the damaged part of the frame, as Blinc's compositor does","message":"**What Blinc does**\nBlinc keeps the last painted frame in a GPU texture (the static layer) and, when little changed, patches it instead of drawing everything: docs/render-architecture.md in the Blinc checkout, the code in crates/blinc_gpu/src/renderer.rs (render_static_layer_damaged) and crates/blinc_app/src/context.rs (damage_union, last_binding_damage_rects, last_css_damage_rects).\n- Damage is the before and after bounds of each primitive that changed, collected as animations tick (motion bindings, CSS animation deltas).\n- The rects are unioned and padded by 4 px so the anti-aliased edges of what moved do not leave a faint outline, then clamped to the layer.\n- One pass loads the cache (LoadOp::Load) with the scissor set to the union. A clear-quad draw with replace blending zeroes the scissor, because a clear load op ignores the scissor, and the primitives whose bounds intersect it are drawn again in order. One pass, not two: on Metal every pass begin loads and stores the whole texture.\n- Effect layers are redrawn separately and composited back inside the scissor; a layer outside it is skipped.\n- It falls back to a full repaint for vector paths, 3D viewports and particles, for any layout change, and when there is no valid cache.\n- Canvas draw closures are a dynamic batch drawn each frame over a blit of the cache; they sit above the static content whatever the order.\n\n**What ashui does now**\nRenderer.draw draws every record of the display list into the frame (a frame texture of its own when a backdrop filter is present, copied to the window at the end), however little changed: all records are encoded in Haxe, backdrops blurred again, canvases replayed.\n\n**Measured on the dashboard (desktop, 2x, no vsync via ASHUI_PRESENT_MODE=immediate)**\nNavigate 8.5 ms a frame, Vehicle 9.4 ms. Skipping one record kind at a time in a scratch renderer: boxes and shadows save about 1.7 ms each on Navigate, canvases about 0.6 ms there and 1.8 ms on Vehicle, text and backdrop blur nothing. Layout takes 2 ms on Navigate and 4 ms on Vehicle, command encoding about 1 ms and 4 ms. So an unthrottled frame is mostly CPU, and a damaged redraw would save the encoding of the records it skips and little of layout. Under the display's own pacing the same pages run at about 50 and 35 fps, which this does not explain.\n\n**Plan**\n- Keep the last frame: draw into the frame texture always, and copy it to the window.\n- Find damage cheaply: compare each record's bytes with last frame's with memcmp (112 floats a record, same count), union the bounds of the records that differ, before and after, padded by the largest shadow and by the 4 px; add the box of each canvas that will be built again (its recorded context differs from the one it was made for, or it moved), and treat a canvas with a paint callback or a 3D scene as always damaged until the scene can say it is unchanged.\n- Grow the union to whole backdrop records and their blur reach, so a blurred box reads fresh pixels all round.\n- Redraw the records that intersect, in order, in one pass with the scissor set, after a clear of the scissor with the window's colour; a canvas's own scissor is cut by it.\n- Fall back to a full frame when the record count or a record's kind changes, a layer record is present, the window is transparent, the size or scale changed, or the damage is over about half the frame.\n- When nothing differs and no canvas is stale, present the last frame again without drawing.\n- Test by drawing a sequence of changes both ways and comparing pixels; ASHUI_DAMAGE=0 turns it off.\n\n**Open**\nHow much it gains is not known until tried: the unthrottled cost is mostly layout and encoding, and a canvas inside a glass panel redraws the whole panel. Several rects as several passes may beat one union.","files":null}]}